= Related Works <relatedworks>
`pygrametl` was introduced as a programmatic @ETL framework and was shown to achieve execution times competitive with established graphical @ETL tools @pygrametl. In the evaluated scenario, it performed similarly to Pentaho Data Integration while using less CPU time and memory, and scaled better as dataset size increased.

Parallel execution was later added through multiprocessing in CPython and multithreading in Jython, allowing @ETL tasks and data partitions to run concurrently @pygrametlPara. That evaluation reported execution-time improvements of 130% to 183% across datasets containing 5 to 100 million facts, with a 53% to 55% increase in CPU time. The results established that using more CPU resources could substantially reduce wall-clock time. This paper builds upon this work by exploring potential performance improvements from modern CPython.
